Give the broker its namespace

`CHALLENGES` exists now. This binds it.

```jsonc
"kv_namespaces": [
  { "binding": "CHALLENGES", "id": "e7fe562219db4d009c5d5a15b68a1097" }
],
```

Item 1.1 on `PAYMENTS-CHECKLIST.md` is struck — the first thing on that list to
go.

## The id is committed on purpose

It names a namespace and grants nothing; reading the data still needs a token
bound to the account. Same class of thing as the R2 bucket name or the Booqable
company id — config, not a credential. `script/test_no_secrets.py` would object
if it were otherwise.

## The comment is rewritten as a warning rather than an instruction

The old text explained why the binding was *absent*. It is not absent anymore,
so that would have read as describing a state the file was no longer in. What
replaces it is the part still worth knowing:

> **NEVER LEAVE `id` AS AN EMPTY STRING. Delete the whole block instead.**

Because the trap survives the fix. If this namespace ever has to be replaced,
the instinct is to blank the id and fill it back in — and that instantly locks
the directory again, refusing `kv namespace create`, the command that would
produce the replacement. Removing the block is safe;
`src/index.js` reports a missing binding as `unconfigured` like every other
unset thing here.

## A correction

I said the namespace would be titled `fcpm-broker-CHALLENGES`, on the basis
that wrangler prefixes with the worker name. It does not — it is titled plainly
**`CHALLENGES`**. Nothing depends on the title, but the record should be right.

## Verified

- `worker/wrangler.jsonc` parses; binding resolves to the id above.
- `npx wrangler kv namespace list` from `worker/` now runs — the directory
  accepts commands again, which it did not before #63.
- The listed namespace's id matches what is committed here.
- Broker tests: **119 passing**.

## What this still does not unblock

The broker cannot deploy yet. `.github/workflows/broker.yml` needs
`CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` as an org secret, and that is a different thing from
being logged in with `wrangler login` — the OAuth session on a laptop is not
readable by GitHub Actions. Item 1.2.
